<!-- DRAFT — two page numbers still missing. Rename to
     themes-gsls-en__c6e87a91.md once they are filled in.

The book is THEMES, Third Year, General Sciences / Life Sciences sections.

Why this file has to exist. The contents table runs across sheets 8, 11, 12,
13 and 15 of the scan with the national-textbook foreword printed in the middle
of it, and it carries no dot leaders at all: the title is on one line and its
page number on the next. Every leader-based rule scores the book zero, so the
parser fell back to locating headings by name, found the three UNIT titles, and
gave the last one everything after page 15 — "Current Concerns" ran pages 15 to
139 of a 139-page book, holding 219 passages and 57 of the subject's 60
questions, while "Natural Phenomena" held 4 passages and "Technology" 14.

Why the page numbers are not optional here. Each unit contains the SAME three
chapter titles — The World Within Us, The World Around Us, New Worlds — so a
title cannot be located in the body: it matches in three places. The printed
number is the only thing that separates them, which is why the two unknowns
below block this file rather than merely blurring it.

THE TWO MISSING NUMBERS. Chapter 4 and Chapter 7 open with a Part A that
carries no page number in the scan, and the surrounding entries only bracket
them:

  Chapter 4  Technology - The World Within Us
             its Part B is printed 54, Part C 56, Part D 58,
             and the chapter before it (New Worlds) starts at 40
             -> so it begins somewhere in 50-53

  Chapter 7  Current Concerns - The World Within Us
             its Part B is printed 90, Part C/D 94 and 97,
             and the chapter before it (New Worlds) starts at 74
             -> so it begins somewhere in 86-89

Open the PDF at the printed page and read the two numbers off the chapter
openings. Do not interpolate them: an offset measured against a guessed page
moves every chapter in the book, which is the failure recorded in
stranded-chapters-root-cause.

The unit name is repeated into each chapter title on purpose. Nine chapters
under three names would reach a student as the same three titles listed three
times over with nothing to tell them apart, which is the duplicate-chapter
complaint this work started from.
